    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-339507",
      "knowledge_point_id": "32cbf366-20bd-5a52-a14f-83add698bf07",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Rational Solutions",
      "problem": "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|-12x+5|}{4x+3}=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "For large negative values of $x,$ we have $|-12x+5|=-12x+5.$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{|-12x+5|}{4x+3}=\\frac{-12x+5}{4x+3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-12x+5}{4x+3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-12x}{x}+\\frac{5}{x})}{(\\frac{4x}{x}+\\frac{3}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-12+\\frac{5}{x})}{(4+\\frac{3}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12+0}{4+0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "-3"
            }
