      "math_academy_id": "q-339421",
      "knowledge_point_id": "32cbf366-20bd-5a52-a14f-83add698bf07",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Rational Solutions",
      "problem": "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{4{x}^{6}-5x}=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{6}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{4{x}^{6}-5x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}})}{(\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}}-\\frac{5x}{{x}^{6}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}})}{(4-\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{4-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
