      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Infinite Solutions",
      "problem": "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4x}{{x}^{2}+x+5}=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4x}{{x}^{2}+5} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-{x}^{6}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{4}+\\frac{4}{x})}{(1+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{4}+0)}{(1+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,-{x}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
