      "problem": "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{3{x}^{4}+2}{{x}^{3}-7x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{3{x}^{4}+2}{{x}^{3}-7x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{7x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(3x+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(3x+0)}{(1-0+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,3x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
