      "math_academy_id": "q-106694",
      "knowledge_point_id": "020c3de3-bcaf-5ade-ba50-2f15e344157d",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Infinite Solutions",
      "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{2x-{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}+5}=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{2x-{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}+5} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2})}{(1+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{0-{x}^{2}}{1-0} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
