      "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function",
      "problem": "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{6{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-2}{2{x}^{4}+3}$ is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-2}{2{x}^{4}+3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(6+\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(2+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+0+0}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=3.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
