    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-339641",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function",
      "problem": "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ for $x>0$ with $f(x)=\\frac{|3-8x|}{4x+1}?$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote is $y=$\n\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|3-8x|=8x-3.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|3-8x|}{4x+1}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8x-3}{4x+1}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x>0$ is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8x-3}{4x+1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8x}{x}-\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{4x}{x}+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(8-\\frac{3}{x})}{(4+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-0}{4+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "2"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "2"
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "moderate",
      "provenance": {
        "activity_question": "question-339641",
        "kp_title": "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function",
