    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-35831",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function",
      "problem": "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{6{x}^{3}+1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{6{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{14}{(6+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{6+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=\\frac{7}{3}.$",
