      "problem": "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{8{x}^{4}+x-2}{6x-5}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{4}+x-2}{6x-5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8{x}^{4}}{x}+\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{2}{x})}{(\\frac{6x}{x}-\\frac{5}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(8{x}^{3}+1-\\frac{2}{x})}{(6-\\frac{5}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(8{x}^{3}+1-0)}{(6-0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(8{x}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote.",
