    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-68072",
      "knowledge_point_id": "bde258b7-1654-5e73-8703-0e42371db8f4",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying When a Rational Function has No Horizontal Asymptote",
      "problem": "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{-7{x}^{4}+3}{2{x}^{2}-4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-7{x}^{4}+3}{2{x}^{2}-4} \\\\  & {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-7{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-7{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(2-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-7{x}^{2}+0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote.",
