      "knowledge_point_id": "32cbf366-20bd-5a52-a14f-83add698bf07",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Rational Solutions",
      "problem": "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty} \\dfrac{3x^4+9x}{6x^4-x^3}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^4.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{4}+9x}{6{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{9x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3+\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-81",
      "knowledge_point_id": "020c3de3-bcaf-5ade-ba50-2f15e344157d",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Infinite Solutions",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -\\infty} \\dfrac{4-5x-2x^3}{x^2+3}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^2.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{4-5x-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-2x-\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{(1+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{-2x+0+0}{1+0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-2x) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-82",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function",
      "problem": "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ with $f(x)=\\dfrac{2x^3+1}{x^3-15x}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{3}-15x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{15x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{15}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+0}{1-0} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-83",
      "knowledge_point_id": "bde258b7-1654-5e73-8703-0e42371db8f4",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying When a Rational Function has No Horizontal Asymptote",
      "problem": "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\dfrac{x^5-10}{x^3-8x^2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{5}-10}{{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{8}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{2}-0}{1-0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote.",
